<rss version="0.92"><channel><item>
            <title>[LU-20319] Maloo auto-vetting shouldn't use "self failures" for history</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-20319</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;If a patch is failing due to self-inflicted issues, I will mark the failure reason as the LU ticket for that patch itself. That is purely for classification reasons to identify if &lt;b&gt;other&lt;/b&gt; test failures are unrelated to the patch, and not to retest the broken patch. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are several patches for &lt;a href="https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-18461" title="Lustre join file re-implementation" class="issue-link" data-issue-key="LU-18461"&gt;LU-18461&lt;/a&gt; that were failing a large number of subtests, and I Associated those failures with the unlanded patch.  Now it appears that &lt;a href="https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-18461" title="Lustre join file re-implementation" class="issue-link" data-issue-key="LU-18461"&gt;LU-18461&lt;/a&gt; is being auto-Associated for nearly every subtest in Maloo, which is impossible in reality since those patches have not landed yet (due to their many failures). &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I think if an LU ticket is Associated to a failure on a patch using that same LU ticket, then it should not be put into the weighting for the general pool of failures in that subtest.  Alternately, if there was a special "SELF" ticket that indicated self-inflicted failure, it would only apply to the patch's specific LU ticket and could not be used for retesting at all. &lt;/p&gt;</description>
